 Chow in B suit with B4, B5, B6 or B6, B7, B8 etc., but you only have B4 and B8, far apart.  \n\nSo possible: aim for two pungs in B4 and B8, plus one pair from T8/T9/T6.  \n\n**Tile safety / opponent discards:**  \nChecking histories:  \n- B4: Previous player played B4 early (their 4th move), B4 hasn\u2019t been seen much recently, 3 remain.  \n- B8: previous player played B8 in move 14, next player didn\u2019t play it. Across played B8? No. But 3 B8 remain.  \n- T8: across played T8 in move 20; next player didn\u2019t play; you have T8 in hand. 2 left unseen from counts.  \n- T9: next player played T9 in moves 4 and 9; across didn\u2019t play; you have T9. 2 left unseen.  \n- T6: across played T6 early (move 4), next player didn\u2019t; you have 2 T6, but one is melded already, so free T6 is maybe not useful unless paired with new T6 draw.  \n\nGiven game state, discarding T6 now may be safe because other players discarded T6 already.  \n\nBut to maintain hand flexibility: keep B4 and B8 for pung possibilities; discard terminal or isolated tile.  \n\n**T9 vs T8 vs T6:**  \nT9 is terminal, might be harder to use if you want All Simples (no terminals/honors) \u2014 but you already have terminal meld (T3 pung) and T5-T7 chow includes middle suit, so All Simples impossible. Also T9 has fewer remaining (1 seen in melds? Wait tile counts show T9:2 unseen means 2 left; 1 is in your hand, so 1 other out there). It could complete chow T7,T8,T9 if you get T7, but T7 only 1 unseen, unlikely.  \n\nT8 could form chow T6,T7,T8 or T7,T8,T9, but again T7 scarce.  \n\nBest: discard the tile least likely to form meld quickly and which is relatively safe.  \n\nLooking at tile counts and opponent exposures:  \n- Across has meld W3,W3,W3 and W4,W5,W6 and B1,B1,B1 \u2014 so across likes pungs and chows in W and B suits, but not many in T suit except maybe T? They played T8 and T7.  \n- Next player\u2019s history has many T suit discards (T9, T1, T5, T2, etc.), so T suit may be safe.  \n\nThus discarding a T suit tile is safer. Among T8, T9, T6: T6 has most unseen (2 left aside from yours), but your free T6 is single, not part of potential chow with your hand because other T\u2019s missing.  \n\nBetter: discard T9 because it\u2019s terminal (scoring patterns you may aim for don\u2019t benefit from extra terminal unless you go for All Terminals, which is impossible).  \n\n**Also consider seat wind/wind pungs:**  \nSeat wind West (F3) \u2014 none in hand. Prevailing wind South (F2) \u2014 none. Not relevant.  \n\n**Final choice reasoning:**  \nDiscard T9 to keep B4 and B8 for potential pung draws, keep T8 and T6 for possible pairing with drawn tiles (T8 could pair with another T8 to make pair for final hand; T6 could pair with new T6 for pair).  \n\nAlso T9 is less flexible for chows given other tiles in hand.  \n\nReason: T9 is a terminal with limited meld potential, safe to discard given opponent histories, and retaining B4/B8 gives better chance at completing two pungs for a winning hand.\n\nAnswer: Play T9"}
